| PX2-S1 | **Spike — is `actions!` expressible in `macro_rules!`?** **Not blocked by Wave 0**; pure feasibility, needs no fingerprint work, can start immediately. `macros.rs:93` records that `macro_rules!` cannot tokenize past `;` inside a body — which is why `CREPE_RULES_SRC` is captured as `stringify!`'d text — and the `actions!` sketch in the palantir PRD §3.2 is semicolon-separated statements inside a body: the same shape that already did not work. Test `actions!` and `functions!` separately; `functions!` bodies are `datalog!`/`scheme!`, already text-shaped, and may be fine while `actions!` is not. **Three outcomes, each changing PX2's size:** (a) it expands — 1a proceeds as written; (b) bodies must be captured as text like Crepe rules — but Crepe rules work because the reasoning crate compiles them at build time, and there is no equivalent home for an Action body that must construct pack-typed memories and edges, so text-capture probably does not survive contact; (c) a proc-macro is required — a new crate, `syn`/`quote` as dependencies (AGENTS.md rule 9 record), and a materially larger PX2. **The semantic stake, not just the implementation cost:** if bodies are not type-checked at pack-compile time, P3's "Actions are typed transforms" weakens and D2's "the pack author cannot bypass the visibility ceiling" stops being a compile-time guarantee. Deliverable is a written decision in the PRD and this row, plus a throwaway `pack!` fixture demonstrating whichever outcome held | palantir-expansion PRD §3.2, §4.1 |
| PX2 | **Depends on OC-PRD (Wave 0)** — adding sections to `PackDef` moves the fingerprint for every pack including dev-v1, so the compatibility split lands first. Pack-registered Actions and Functions: `actions!` / `functions!` / `guidance!` sections on the `pack!` macro, macro-generated `inventory::submit!` registration (one registry, R-P1/R-P2 preserved), audit + preflight + visibility-ceiling enforcement, generated Function-SLO bench harness, `--dump-tools`, `--dump-fingerprint` | palantir-expansion PRD §3.2, §4.1-4.3 | Accepted — post-v1, not release-blocking |
| PX3 | Seam inventory (`cargo xtask seam-inventory`) + a conformance suite per seam, the gate asserting a bijection between declared seams and green suites rather than a count (a legitimate new seam costs a suite, not an edit to a constant). Includes the `ChangeLog` seam, D25. **Note:** the existing `storage-conformance` gate runs `cargo test -p exocortex-storage` plus the Falkor integration suite when `FALKOR_URL` is set — two different test sets against two backends, not one shared corpus run against every `Storage` impl. There are three impls (Falkor, InMemory, `NoBackendStorage` in the client), and the third is where the SR-PRD read-back defects lived. The gate's name currently overstates it | palantir-expansion PRD §3.3 | Accepted — post-v1; the naming finding is worth folding into Round 6 verification |
| PX4 | Mintlify adapter end-to-end on a real `loanlight-engineering/docs` checkout: >=30 memories + >=50 edges through the live ingest path as `ProducerKind::DocsAdapter`, idempotent rerun, `RejectCode::UnknownType` exercised against real input | palantir-expansion PRD §3.4 | Accepted — post-v1, not release-blocking |
| PX5 | Read-only object explorer at `/explorer` on the node binary: six views, bearer auth, `VisibilityContext`-filtered at every render, no JS build step | palantir-expansion PRD §3.5 | Accepted — post-v1; depends on PX6 for the provenance view |
| PX6 | Kernel catalogue <-> operation registry bijection: `GetChain`, `ExplainEdge`, and `RetractEdge` are declared in `exocortex-kernel/src/actions.rs` / `functions.rs` with names and budgets and have no registered operation. `operations.rs:3` already documents `retract_edge` as living in that module; it does not. Implement all three | palantir-expansion PRD §3.3 S6 | Accepted — post-v1. Smallest item; `soft_delete_relationship` already exists under `RetractEdge` |

**Wave assignment for the PX rows above:** OC-PRD is Wave 0 and blocks PX2 —
the fingerprint moves once, deliberately, before pack verbs force it to move
by accident. PX2 and PX6 are Wave 1 and
unblock the rest (PX6 is the smallest item in the plan and PX5 depends on
it); PX1 and PX3 are Wave 1; PX4 is Wave 2, alongside D1 and D13 — the
docs run is one producer among several rather than the headline; PX5 is
Wave 3.

### Vertical pack candidates

Not committed work — candidates, listed so the pack strategy is visible and
argued rather than improvised. All of them are gated on PX1 proving an outside
author can ship a pack, and on D13, because every one of these domains is
multi-source and unjoined data is not an ontology.

The selection principle: **regulated domains arrive with their ontology already
written.** ECOA/Reg B reason codes, SNOMED/ICD/LOINC, CDISC SDTM, DO-178C
traceability, MITRE ATT&CK — someone already did the taxonomy work and made it
mandatory. A pack encodes a taxonomy the customer is already required to follow,
versions it, diffs it, and proves which version was in force on a given date.
That is the pack seam's actual market, and it is why the fingerprint and
bi-temporal work is load-bearing rather than decorative.

| ID | Wave | Vertical | Why it fits | Gate |
|----|------|----------|-------------|------|
| VP1 | **1** | Mortgage / lending underwriting | Adverse-action notices legally require a stated reason; rulebooks vary by lender and change over time, so "which rules governed this file" is a bi-temporal question with a compliance consequence | Already in flight as PX1 |
| VP2 | **2** | Software engineering | Git + Linear + docs + incidents is a graph every org over ~100 engineers has and nobody solves deterministically. Uniquely, it demos on the prospect's own repo with no data leaving the machine | D18, D19 |
| VP3 | **4** | Clinical trials / pharma | 21 CFR Part 11 and ALCOA require attributable, contemporaneous, never-destructively-edited records — a prose description of provenance over an append-only bi-temporal store. Protocol amendments are ontology versions; deviations are typed findings against the version in force | PX1, D13 |
| VP4 | **4** | Security detection engineering and IR | Detection rules as versioned ontology; post-incident review is a bi-temporal query. Buyers are structurally hostile to both data egress and LLMs in a defensible decision path | PX1, D13 |
| VP5 | **5** | Aerospace / safety-critical engineering | Requirements traceability is a typed graph with provenance and nothing else; auditors ask what the trace looked like at certification time. Air-gapped deployment is mandatory, which is a feature here | PX1, D13, air-gap story |
| VP6 | **5** | Utilities / industrial | Asset ontologies, maintenance history, failure causality, "what was the configuration when it failed" | PX1, D13, D14 |

Explicitly poor fits, recorded so they are not revisited: consumer and marketing
analytics (volume-first, semantics-thin, nobody defends a decision three years
later); anything whose value is prediction rather than record (no LLM, no ML —
that is not our fight); petabyte telemetry (wrong tier, see D14); and raw-prose
domains such as PDF contract piles, where the no-LLM rule means we can only
ingest what is already structured.

### Training-corpus direction (open, not yet accepted)

A candidate reframing worth recording: Exocortex as the layer that **ingests
broadly, validates deterministically, and emits training-ready corpora** — the
typed ontology as the corpus schema, the validators as the quality gate, and
bi-temporality plus provenance as the two things training-data pipelines
generally cannot produce (leak-free temporal splits, and per-record lineage).
Under that framing the `no LLM inside` invariant stops being a constraint and
becomes the product claim: the thing preparing the data is the thing that cannot
hallucinate. D22, D23, and D24 are its concrete pieces.

Three tensions to resolve before this is accepted as direction rather than an
idea:

1. **Scale stops being deferrable.** Corpora are millions to billions of rows
   against a 100k benchmark. D14 moves from "wait for a deployment that demands
   it" to the primary axis, and that reopens storage-architecture decisions this
   plan has deliberately postponed.
2. **"All the information" implies unstructured sources**, and deterministic
   extraction only works on already-structured ones. That forces D23 — and
   option (a) is the version that keeps the invariant intact.
3. **It is a second product with a different buyer**, different competitors, and
   a different scale envelope from the ontology framing. The substrate is shared;
   the go-to-market is not. Pick deliberately rather than drift.

