## Sequenced (post-v1)

Nothing here is deferred to a v2 that never arrives. Every item is accepted
work with a wave; a wave is a **dependency and value ordering, not a
schedule** — no dates, no durations, no estimates. Work starts on a wave
when the prior wave's blocking items are closed and Round 6 is shut.
IDs are stable: review rounds and PRDs cite them, so they are never
renumbered, only re-waved.

- **Wave 0 — ontology compatibility.** How two components decide they may
  exchange data at all. It comes first because Wave 1 moves the fingerprint
  whether or not anyone planned for it, and Wave 0 is the difference between a
  managed change and a fleet-wide surprise. `OC-PRD`.
- **Wave 1 — ontology verbs and modularity.** The pack seam becomes a
  product: typed domain verbs, a second pack, and the seam set that keeps
  both honest. Everything else reads better once this lands.
- **Wave 2 — data breadth.** The claim that matters most and the one the
  repo is weakest on: point Exocortex at data that is not a coding
  session. Structured-source adapters plus the deterministic half of
  entity resolution, which is what makes a graph of documents into a model
  of a world.
- **Wave 3 — surface and operability.** Make the graph visible and the
  node observable.
- **Wave 4 — scale.** Prove the numbers past 100k before anyone bets an
  org on them.
- **Wave 5 — multi-tenant and federated.** The capabilities that only
  matter once more than one org depends on a deployment.

| ID | Wave | Item | PRD |
|----|------|------|-----|
| D1 | **2** | *(D21 steps a+d)* Iceberg / Delta / parquet-dir adapter workers — **undeferred**. Already unblocked (B8+B9+A1 closed) and the single largest gap between what the README claims and what the repo ingests: today every producer is a coding session, a docs page, or the analytics worker. Structured-source ingest is what makes "point it at your data" true | §18.4, §24 |
| D2 | **1** | Second-pack demo — **superseded by PX1**: promoted from deferral to accepted scope as `exocortex-pack-mortgage-v1`, with the non-primary-author rule as its acceptance test | §23.26, §24 |
| D3 | **5** | Federation | §24 |
| D4 | **4** | Embedding fine-tuning | §24 |
| D5 | **4** | Ingest-time SimilarTo seeding (`SimilarityCosine`) | §24 q12 (lean: no) |
| D6 | **3** | OTel export (R-O3) — opentelemetry dep decision | §19, round-2 W14 |
| D7 | **3** | Contradiction-resolution op (§23 #13) | §23 #13, round-2 H14 |
| D8 | **4** | ABSTRACT row-writing variant (ontology shape TBD) | §12.1 step 4, round-2 H9 |
| D9 | **4** | 100-user visibility fuzz + concurrent-region/CrossRegion suites | §23 #21/#22, round-2 H10 |
| D10 | **4** | Wire-protocol throughput fan-out + additive-change rollout tests | §23 #23, round-2 H10 |
| D11 | **4** | Partition-and-heal live run (leader-kill convergence OBSERVED: 4/4 in 1290-1448ms; the partition leg remains manual) | §3 M5 AC, round-2 H10 |
| D12 | **1** | Round-3 accepted residue (R1/R2 above): bridge reconcile loop, cursor fsync, checksum preimage hardening, persistent batch-dedup, WAL replay rebuild — **superseded in part**: persistent batch-dedup closed by W7, WAL replay rebuild+sign closed by W1 (the drain signs fresh, never replays stored signatures) | round-3 review M3/M6-M15 |
| D13 | **2** | **Deterministic entity resolution** — resolve the same real-world entity across producers by shared external identity (`ExternalKey`, typed entities as convergent join points), not by fuzzy matching. Explicitly NOT the ML half: no corpus, no labelled set, no model. Without this the graph is a collection of documents rather than a model of a world, which is the core ontology claim. Fuzzy/probabilistic ER is a separate later item, and `NearDuplicateApplicant` stays a Dreams-computed proposal in the meantime | palantir-expansion PRD §5; core PRD §24 |
| D14 | **4** | Org-scale benchmarks past the existing 100k suite (10M memories) — forces the embedding-store decision, so it waits for a deployment that demands it rather than leading one | palantir-expansion PRD §5, §24 |
| D15 | **5** | Row-level ACLs beyond the five visibility labels (inheritance chains, an authorization DSL) | palantir-expansion PRD §5, §24 |
| D16 | **5** | Ontology branching / PRs against the ontology — only meaningful once a third pack is proposed by someone outside the org | palantir-expansion PRD §5, §24 |
| D17 | **2** | Probabilistic / fuzzy entity resolution (the half D13 excludes): corpus, labelled set, evaluation harness. Sequenced after D13 proves the deterministic join carries most of the value | palantir-expansion PRD §5, §24 |
| D18 | **2** | *(D21 steps a+d)* **Git-history adapter** — commits, authors, files, PRs into `Fix` / `Problem` / `CodePattern` / `Technology`, which `exocortex-pack-dev-v1` already declares. No auth, no network, fully deterministic, offline-testable, and self-evidencing: it runs against this repository. Cheapest real adapter available and the strongest zero-friction demo, so it lands ahead of D1 | adapter roadmap; §18.4 |
| D19 | **2** | *(D21 steps a+d)* **SaaS-API adapter family** — Linear/Jira first, then GitHub, then Salesforce/ServiceNow. Highest semantic density of any source: these systems already carry relationships (issue→assignee→team, PR→commit→file), so edges are transcribed rather than inferred. Linear and GitHub join directly to D18's git graph on the same entities | adapter roadmap; §18.4 |
| D20 | **3** | *(D21 steps a+d)* **CDC adapters** — Postgres logical replication first, then MySQL binlog / Debezium. Structurally different from table sources: continuous rather than batch-with-cursor, deletes arrive as first-class events (a clean match for soft-delete and `valid_until`), primary keys map onto `ExternalKey`, and source ordering must reconcile with LSN assignment. This is the category that makes integration of *operational* systems real | adapter roadmap; §18.4 |
| D21 | **2** | **Adapter contract** — `docs/prd/adapter-contract-prd.md`. Three gaps that D1/D18-D20 all hit and none of them owns: (a) **projection discipline** — a table can hold a billion rows against a 100k benchmark, so an adapter ingests a bounded projection, never a table, and the contract must say so; (b) **local validation vs R-I1/R-I4** — the SDK links wire only and never the kernel, so rejections are discovered at the server, which is fine for 70 docs pages and untenable at table volume; needs either a server-side dry-run generalizing `preflight_wrapup` or the rulebook shipped to adapters as data; (c) **source-schema evolution policy** — `schema_hash` and `mapping_version` model it, nothing defines behaviour when a column is added or retyped. Blocks D1, D19, D20 — those three should not ship before its steps (a) projection and (d) schema policy land, or each invents its own answer and the three disagree. Its rulebook-as-data step depends on OC-PRD Wave 0, because a validation manifest is only verifiable if it is stamped with the compatibility fingerprint | `docs/prd/adapter-contract-prd.md` |
| D22 | **3** | **Training-corpus export** — materialize the graph as JSONL for training and evaluation. Two properties fall out of the existing design rather than needing to be built: bi-temporal validity yields **temporally-clean splits** (everything the graph believed as of time T, with no future leakage), and provenance plus `ExternalKey` plus the audit ledger yield a **per-record lineage manifest** answering where each row came from. Dreams' MCR² and `SimilarTo` machinery already covers corpus dedup. Adjacent to the shipped `--export` work | training direction (below) |
| D23 | **3** | **LLM boundary decision** — the `no LLM inside` invariant (R-D6/CR-19, `cargo xtask no-llm`, AGENTS.md rule 1) is enforced by CI and cited as a differentiator throughout the PRDs. Two very different things are called "LLM integration" and this row exists to force the choice explicitly rather than let a gate get quietly disabled: **(a) LLM as an external producer** — extraction runs out-of-process in an adapter, emits typed drafts through the same signed ingest path, is stamped `Provenance::Extracted` with a distinguishing `ProducerKind`, and is filterable and revocable at query time. Costs nothing architecturally; the gate greps this workspace, not adapter repos; the store stays deterministic. **(b) LLM inside the node** — semantic search, natural-language query, generative reads. Breaks R-D6/CR-19, deletes the differentiator, and changes what the product is. Option (a) is available today; option (b) requires an explicit invariant reversal recorded here and in `docs/MILESTONE_REPORT.md` | direction decision |
| D24 | **3** | **Rights and consent provenance** — if corpora leave the system for training, "may this be trained on" becomes a first-class field: licence, consent basis, retention limit, redaction state, per source and per record. Not modelled today. Gates D22 for any corpus that leaves the org | training direction (below) |
| D25 | **1** | **Name the change-feed seam (`ChangeLog`)** — the one major subsystem with no written contract. Append-at-LSN lives in `cluster/src/node.rs`, feed mount and auth in `server/src/sse.rs`, consumption and gap detection in `client/src/sync.rs`: three crates holding three partial ideas of one contract, which is exactly how CS1 happened (an empty replay ring answering "you are current" instead of 409 Resync, silently dropping every invalidation in the gap). Ships as a trait with four operations over today's in-memory ring — no behaviour change, no new dependency, no fingerprint move — plus a conformance suite landing as the seventh seam in PX3's inventory. **The case is CS1, not scale** | palantir-expansion PRD §3.3 S7 |
| D26 | **4** | **Broker-backed change log** — swap a durable, replayable broker in behind D25's port. **Redpanda** as the reference (single binary, Kafka-wire-compatible, no JVM and no ZooKeeper, so it sits with the single-binary ethos rather than against it); NATS JetStream the alternative. Apache Kafka itself is rejected: the operational surface contradicts the product's own deployment claim. Two things must be decided before any implementation, not during: **(a) LSN partitioning** — `next_lsn_block` hands out a contiguous, globally ordered range and the client's `since_lsn` resync depends on that order being real, while a partitioned log orders only within a partition; a single partition is almost certainly right at this tier and must be written down, because the pressure to "just add partitions" arrives under load and not while reading a PRD. **(b) The in-process implementation stays the default forever** — a broker is opt-in, or standalone mode and the single-binary claim both die. Gated on genuine scale pressure or on the D22 training direction being accepted, whichever comes first | D25; D22; D14 |
